  "prompt": "prompts/medicine_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B004",
        "laureates": [
          "Douglas R. Lowy",
          "Ian H. Frazer",
          "John T. Schiller"
        ],
        "citation": "for virus-like particle vaccines that prevent HPV infection and related cancers"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B010",
    "B029"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B004 most completely joins a defined virological and technological advance to a mature, direct, and exceptionally important preventive medical result. A narrowly framed VLP-vaccine citation distinguishes this achievement from establishing HPV cancer causation, and Lowy, Frazer, and Schiller remain the most coherent prize-compatible three-person account of the supplied central vaccine advance.",
    "case_against": "The remaining reservation is historical delimitation: foundational papillomavirus biology, VLP work, clinical development, and implementation cannot all be absorbed into one award. Prior recognition of HPV causation makes disciplined wording essential. B010 offers an equally mature therapeutic transformation and B029 exceptional conceptual depth, but neither displaces B004 because B004 currently has the more bounded discovery-to-medicine narrative and allocation of credit.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "wahren-herlenius-marie",
        "point": "Wahren-Herlenius retains GLP-1 physiology and therapeutic application as her preferred configuration, arguing that Holst, Mojsov, and Drucker can recognize the linked physiological and translational achievement without claiming all pharmaceutical credit.",
        "response": "I agree that B010 is a major, mature achievement and that careful wording can avoid overstating the trio's role in drug development. The residual attribution problem nevertheless extends across peptide discovery, receptor biology, physiology, and pharmaceutical translation. In comparison, B004 can be more tightly limited to the VLP vaccine advance while retaining extraordinary demonstrated preventive consequence."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "perlmann-thomas",
        "point": "Perlmann emphasizes that B004 is strongest if the VLP-vaccine principle and its preventive outcome are kept separate from broader papillomavirus biology and the previously recognized HPV-causation discovery.",
        "response": "I concur. This delimitation answers the principal distinctness concern without diminishing the separate scientific and medical significance of prophylactic VLP vaccination. It also prevents the citation from implying credit for subsequent implementation, while leaving the proposed trio attached to the core achievement under consideration."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "svenningsson-per",
        "point": "Svenningsson considers B029 an exceptionally strong alternative because the B- and T-lymphocyte lineage discovery is foundational, mature, and compactly attributable to Miller and Cooper.",
        "response": "I agree that B029 has unusual conceptual clarity and a compelling two-person attribution. It remains a leading alternative, but B004 better combines a delimited mechanistic advance with a sustained preventive intervention against infection and cancer; on common criteria that direct medical consequence narrowly prevails for me."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether Lowy, Frazer, and Schiller most completely capture the foundational VLP vaccine contribution within the three-person limit.",
      "Whether the final citation can remain sufficiently narrow to distinguish VLP vaccination from prior recognition of HPV causation, clinical development, and implementation."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No candidate-related connection is apparent from the supplied profile. The profile records eight coauthored works with committee member Rickard Sandberg, a count not independently audited."
  }
}
